%I A400411 #10 Oct 07 2026 14:34:13
%S A400411 0,0,0,0,30,0,2304,0,218680,395940,28223770,0,4880546113,3790165176,
%T A400411 1095264882758,0
%N A400411 Number of deals of n cards numbered 1 to n between two players for which the card game War never ends.
%C A400411 The game: the cards 1..n are dealt alternately to two players, player 1 first. On each turn both players turn over their top card. The higher card wins, and the winner puts the winning card and then the losing card at the bottom of their pile. A player with no cards loses. All cards are different, so there are no ties. This is the version of War studied by Spivey. The play is fixed by the deal, so a game that never ends eventually repeats a position and from then on goes around the same cycle of positions forever.
%C A400411 The count is the same for any way of dealing that gives each player a fixed number of cards, since every such rule produces each pair of starting piles exactly once. For odd n it also does not matter which player gets the extra card, since swapping the players turns an endless game into an endless game.
%C A400411 a(n)/n! is the probability that a random deal never ends; Spivey's Question 3 asks for this probability, and he gave a(10) = 395940. For odd n the share of endless deals grows: 25.0%, 45.7%, 60.3%, 70.7%, 78.4% and 83.8% for n = 5, 7, ..., 15. For even n it is 0 or small: 10.9% for n = 10 and 4.35% for n = 14.
%C A400411 Spivey showed that for every n not in A029744 some deal never ends, so a(n) > 0 for those n. For the terms of A029744 up to 16, n = 1, 2, 3, 4, 6, 8, 12, 16, no position of the game lies on a cycle at all, so a(n) = 0. For n <= 4 this is due to Spivey, and Delahaye and Mathieu found no endless deal for n = 6, 8, 12. For n = 6, 8, 12, 16 it follows from a search of every position in which player 1 has card n on top, since every cycle passes through such a position, up to swapping the players (card n never loses, so in an endless game it keeps coming back to the top of its holder's pile). Spivey's Question 2 asks whether any cycle exists for these n; if none does, a(n) = 0 for every n in A029744.
%C A400411 For odd n, every endless game falls into a cycle of lcm(n+1, 2a, 2b) turns, where just before a turn won by player 1, player 1 holds 2a cards and player 2 holds 2b+1 cards, with a, b >= 1 and a + b = (n-1)/2. Every such length occurs. For n = 13 the lengths are 42, 56 and 70.
%C A400411 In the cycles constructed by Spivey, the two players take turns winning runs of the same length, a power of 2. Every cycle with at most 13 cards is of this kind. The first exceptions are at n = 14: counting a cycle and the one with the players swapped as one, 720 of the 26640 cycles of 14 cards are not of Spivey's kind, all of length 96, and 61184620 of the a(14) endless deals end in one of them. Cycles not of Spivey's kind, entered from ordinary deals, exist for every n = m*2^v with m == 3 (mod 4), m >= 7 and v >= 1 (n = 14, 22, 28, 30, 38, 44, 46, ...). Conjecture: they exist for no other n.
%C A400411 Conjecture: for every n, the length of every cycle is divisible by n + A006519(n). This holds for every cycle with at most 16 cards, for every cycle when n is odd, for every cycle of Spivey's kind, and for every cycle in the known infinite families of the other kind.
%C A400411 Each term was computed by at least two independently written programs. The Python program below is practical only for small n; a(11)-a(16) were computed with GPU programs. For n = 16 two of them searched all 19615115520000 positions with card 16 on top of player 1's pile.
%D A400411 Jean-Paul Delahaye and Philippe Mathieu, Jeu de la bataille : à la recherche des parties infinies, Pour la Science, No. 567 (January 2025), 80-85.
%H A400411 Manan Bhatia, Byron Chin, Nitya Mani and Elchanan Mossel, <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2302.03535">When will (game) wars end?</a>, arXiv:2302.03535 [math.CO], 2023-2024; Amer. Math. Monthly 133 (2026), 35-46.
%H A400411 Jean-Paul Delahaye and Philippe Mathieu, <a href="https://www.cristal.univ-lille.fr/~jdelahay/pls/1995/030.pdf">La bataille, enfin analysée</a>, Pour la Science, No. 215 (September 1995), 100-103.
%H A400411 Jean-Paul Delahaye and Philippe Mathieu, <a href="https://github.com/cristal-smac/bataille">bataille</a>, GitHub repository (code and data for their 2025 article).
%H A400411 Tanya Khovanova and Atharva Pathak, <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2202.00473">Combinatorial aspects of the card game War</a>, arXiv:2202.00473 [math.CO], 2022; Australas. J. Combin. 91 (2025), 301-325.
%H A400411 Evgeny Lakshtanov and Vera Roshchina, <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/1007.1371">On finiteness in the card game of War</a>, arXiv:1007.1371 [math.DS], 2010-2011; Amer. Math. Monthly 119 (2012), 318-323.
%H A400411 Daniel Okwor, <a href="https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.22957686">Endless Games of War: Counting the Deals That Never End</a>, Zenodo, 2026.
%H A400411 Daniel Okwor, <a href="https://github.com/OrygnsCode/endless-games-of-war">Programs and data for endless games of War</a>, GitHub repository, 2026.
%H A400411 Michael Z. Spivey, <a href="https://math.colgate.edu/~integers/kg2/kg2.pdf">Cycles in War</a>, Integers 10 (2010), #G02, 747-764.
%H A400411 Wikipedia, <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/War_(card_game)">War (card game)</a>.
%e A400411 a(5) = 30: of the 120 deals of 5 cards, 30 never end, and each of them ends in a cycle of 6 turns. One of them is the deck in order 1, 2, 3, 4, 5. Player 1 gets 1, 3, 5 and player 2 gets 2, 4 (top card first), and the piles go
%e A400411   (1,3,5 | 2,4) -> (3,5 | 4,2,1) -> (5 | 2,1,4,3) -> (5,2 | 1,4,3) -> (2,5,1 | 4,3)
%e A400411   -> (5,1 | 3,4,2) -> (1,5,3 | 4,2) -> (5,3 | 2,4,1) -> (3,5,2 | 4,1) -> (5,2 | 1,4,3),
%e A400411 so from the fourth position on, the game repeats every 6 turns.
%o A400411 (Python)
%o A400411 from itertools import permutations
%o A400411 from collections import deque
%o A400411 def a(n):  # deals of cards 1..n for which War (winning card, then losing card, under the winner's pile) never ends
%o A400411     count = 0
%o A400411     for deal in permutations(range(1, n + 1)):
%o A400411         p1, p2, seen = deque(deal[0::2]), deque(deal[1::2]), set()
%o A400411         while p1 and p2:
%o A400411             state = (tuple(p1), tuple(p2))
%o A400411             if state in seen:
%o A400411                 count += 1
%o A400411                 break
%o A400411             seen.add(state)
%o A400411             x, y = p1.popleft(), p2.popleft()
%o A400411             if x > y:
%o A400411                 p1.extend((x, y))
%o A400411             else:
%o A400411                 p2.extend((y, x))
%o A400411     return count
%o A400411 print([a(n) for n in range(1, 11)])
%Y A400411 Cf. A000375, A006519, A029744, A110017, A240567.
